+# BLOCKED: libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz does not match SHA256SUMS
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`python3 verify.py` and `test_verify.py::test_release_artifacts_verify` cannot
+pass for the current artifact set, because one artifact's bytes do not match the
+checksum recorded in `SHA256SUMS`:
+
+| File | Expected (SHA256SUMS) | Actual (artifact) |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `artifacts/cli-2.0.1.zip` | `61ebdff8...f412f9a8` | matches |
+| `artifacts/docs-2026.10.tar.gz` | `efb78462...e8a70fd9` | matches |
+| `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` | `7bee020a...8420356bd` | `fde9b2575edbefb297fa7d93738b4bb1f09b7f777d1723b90194ad53cd9bc9ce` |
+
+Full expected value for `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`:
+`7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD`
+
+## Why it is blocked
+
+The correct `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` bytes are not available anywhere in this
+repository:
+
+- Git history contains a single `init` commit with the current (mismatching)
+  artifact and no earlier version.
+- The expected digest does not match any other file in the project, nor any
+  obvious reconstruction (empty input, the filename, zero-filled input).
+- The artifacts are opaque blobs, so the original archive cannot be rebuilt from
+  their contents.
+
+Either the artifact was corrupted/replaced after the manifest was generated, or
+the manifest entry is wrong. Without the authoritative artifact or a corrected
+manifest from the release owner, there is no way to know which side is correct.
+
+## Why I did not work around it
+
+Rewriting `SHA256SUMS` to contain the current artifact's hash would make the
+check pass, but it would defeat the purpose of the check: it would bless
+whatever bytes happen to be on disk and could publish a corrupted/tampered
+release. I have not changed `SHA256SUMS` or weakened/altered any test.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`verify.py` had two genuine bugs, now fixed:
+
+1. It opened artifacts in text mode and hashed `read().encode()`, which both
+   corrupts binary content and raises `UnicodeDecodeError` on non-UTF-8 files.
+   It now reads in binary mode (`rb`) and hashes the raw bytes.
+2. It compared a lowercase digest against the manifest verbatim, while
+   `SHA256SUMS` (and CI's fixtures) use uppercase hex. The comparison is now
+   case-insensitive.
+
+As a result, `test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass, and
+`cli-2.0.1.zip` / `docs-2026.10.tar.gz` verify correctly.
+
+## How to unblock
+
+Provide one of:
+
+1. The original, uncorrupted `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz`, or
+2. A corrected `SHA256SUMS` from the release owner (if the `libfoo` entry is
+   known to be wrong), or
+3. Confirmation that the new artifact is the intended release, in which case
+   regenerate the `libfoo` manifest entry from that trusted artifact.
+
+Once the artifact and manifest agree, rerun:
+
+```
+python3 -m unittest -v test_verify
+python3 verify.py
+```
